<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morgantina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Morgantina is an ancient city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> near Aidone, occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicels">Sicels</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> to the Roman period. Captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ducetius">Ducetius</a> in the 5th century BCE, it flourished under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-ii-of-syracuse">Hieron II of Syracuse</a> and was sacked by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 211 BCE. Excavations since 1955 have revealed its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a> and houses, and looted treasures from the site were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from American museums.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morgantina.jpg" alt="Morgantina" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlexanderVanLoon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Morgantina_seen_from_western_hill_AvL.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The site lies in the province of Enna, sixty kilometres from the Ionian coast and two kilometres north-east of Aidone. It covers a two-kilometre ridge called Serra Orlando and a neighbouring hill to the north-east, Cittadella. The first major settlement, on Cittadella, lasted from about 1000/900 to about 450 BCE; the second, on Serra Orlando, from about 450 BCE to about 50 CE. Kenan Erim identified Serra Orlando as Morgantina from coins bearing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word HISPANORUM and from passages of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. Strabo says the city was founded by the Morgetes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-calabria">Rhegium</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ducetius">Ducetius</a> took it in 459 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> records that at the Congress of Gela Syracuse gave Morgantina to Kamarina for an indemnity. Dionysios of Syracuse recaptured it in 396 BCE, and in 317 BCE the exiled Agathocles found help there to return to Syracuse. Part of Hiero II's kingdom when he became a Roman vassal in 263 BCE, it went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in 214 BCE and in 211 BCE was the last Sicilian town to fall to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, which gave it to Spanish mercenaries. Eunus, leader of the First Servile War, died there in 133 BCE. Strabo wrote that by his time the city had ceased to exist.</p><p>Its grape, the vitis murgentina, was praised by Cato, Columella and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a>. Luigi Pappalardo dug the first trenches in 1884 and Paolo Orsi in 1912. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> began a major project in 1955 under Erik Sjöqvist and Richard Stillwell, joined in the 1950s by King Gustaf VI Adolf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. From 1978 Malcolm Bell III of the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> led the work towards publication in the Morgantina Studies series. Excavations of the North Baths since 2003 have found one of the earliest examples of dome and barrel vault construction, and a 3rd-century BCE bouleuterion stood west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Finds from the site are shown in the Archaeological Museum of Aidone, opened in 1980 in a 17th-century former Capuchin monastery. By the 1980s works looted from or near Morgantina had been sold through the dealer Robin Symes: an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> bought by the J. Paul Getty Museum, parts of two acroliths bought by Maurice Tempelsman and the Morgantina treasure bought by the Metropolitan Museum of Art. All were returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and are displayed in the museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morgantina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He took <b>Morgantina</b> and Inessa and attacked Acragan and Syracusan territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/sicel-league">Sicel League</a></li></ul></section>
